{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Module.LinearMap.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.Bochner.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Perm.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost_can_separate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost_can_separate","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L216-L228","doc":"**The block cost CAN separate two words**, unlike the crossing count,\nprovided their per-block vectors differ in a way the weights do not cancel.\nExhibited concretely: the exclusion in `LetterSumBlindness` does not extend to\nthis class.\n\nNot a claim that it separates the four gluon candidates — that is a\nmeasurement, and it is not made here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost_apply","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L209-L214","doc":"The block cost of a word is its per-block vector's weighted total —\nthe `W`-indexed form of §1, and the statement that the socket receives the\nsame object §1 decomposes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockCost","kind":"def","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L201-L207","doc":"A cost assembled from per-block data. `blocks w` is the per-block vector\n`t_λ(w)` of the word `w`; the cost is its `y`-weighted total. The point is the\nTYPE: this is a `W → R`, so it drops into `SDPSelected` / `FrobeniusSelected`\nin place of `fun w => (w.length : R)` with no change to those structures. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.pairSum_not_perm_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.pairSum_not_perm_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L179-L196","doc":"**A separation-dependent pair sum is NOT permutation-invariant.**\n\nThis is the escape, and it is the whole reason a double integral can do what a\nletter sum provably cannot. `LetterSumBlindness.letterSum_perm_invariant`\nshows any `Σ_letters f(letter)` is constant on the permutation orbit; here a\nconcrete kernel and a concrete transposition give different values.\n\nThe mechanism is worth stating because it is the design constraint on any\nreplacement cost: permuting the letters does not change the multiset, but it\nDOES change which pairs sit at which separation, so a kernel that reads the\nseparation sees the reordering. A pair kernel that ignored `d` and was\nsymmetric in its two letters would be permutation-invariant again and would\nbuy nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.pairSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.pairSum","kind":"def","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L172-L177","doc":"Sum over ordered pairs of letters, weighted by a kernel that sees both\nletters AND their separation along the word. The O'Hara integrand is of this\nshape, with `K` carrying the `1/d²` factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.headPairs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.headPairs","kind":"def","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L165-L170","doc":"Pairs of a distinguished head letter with each later letter, carrying the\nSEPARATION `d` between them. This is the discrete shadow of the O'Hara\nintegrand's dependence on `|s - t|`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.integral_blockDecomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.integral_blockDecomposition","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L143-L160","doc":"**The block decomposition at the Bochner integral.**\n\nThe integrand is a finite `y`-weighted sum of per-block integrands, each\nintegrable; the integral of the sum is the weighted sum of the integrals. With\n`α := S¹ × S¹ ∖ diag` and `μ` the product measure this is exactly the\n`E_cat` decomposition, with no hypothesis beyond integrability of each block's\nintegrand.\n\nIntegrability is a genuine hypothesis and is stated, not assumed away: the\nO'Hara integrand is regularised precisely so that it is integrable across the\ndiagonal, and on the flat-torus geodesic realisation it is identically zero\nthere (`qbeta_bring_ohara_bridge_probe`, check [C2]). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockVector_strictly_finer_than_total","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockVector_strictly_finer_than_total","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L124-L136","doc":"**A per-block VECTOR is strictly finer than its total.** Two block\ndecompositions can have the same total and different components, so a cost\nbuilt from the vector can separate cases the scalar cannot.\n\nThis is the precise sense in which decomposing `E_cat` over partitions buys\nsomething: it is not merely a re-writing. Exhibited concretely rather than\nasserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.total_eq_sum_blockCosts","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.total_eq_sum_blockCosts","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L114-L122","doc":"The total is exactly the sum of the per-block costs: with\n`t_λ := y_λ * I (f λ)`, we get `E_cat = Σ_λ t_λ`. This is the Lean form of the\nassembly identity `tr_M = Σ_λ t_λ` that `computations/nucleus_sdp_assembly.py`\nasserts numerically, and it makes the per-block vector and the scalar total two\nviews of one object. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockDecomposition_of_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.ECatBlockDecomposition.blockDecomposition_of_linear","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ECatBlockDecomposition.lean#L93-L112","doc":"**The block decomposition of `E_cat`, derived rather than assumed.**\n\n`I` is the integral, presented as an arbitrary linear functional; `y` are the\nWedderburn block weights `y_λ`; `f λ` is the per-block integrand\n`χ_λ[(Δ(γ(s)) ⊗ Δ(γ(t))) ⋆_q ‖…‖]`. The conclusion\n\n    I (Σ_λ y_λ • f λ)  =  Σ_λ y_λ * I (f λ)\n\nsays the total energy is the `y`-weighted sum of per-block integrals, so the\nper-block vector `t_λ := y_λ * I (f λ)` is a REFINEMENT of `E_cat` and not a\ndifferent quantity.\n\nStated over a generic commutative ring with `I` an arbitrary linear map: the\ndecomposition is a fact about linearity, so it cannot be an artefact of the\nmeasure, of the `q`-deformation, or of the particular blocks. ","signatureChunk":0}]}